# Chapters
h('Chapter 1  Introduction'); h('1.1 Introduction',2); para('Crop diseases can reduce yield and quality, while early recognition is difficult when symptoms are subtle or timely access to plant-health expertise is limited. Image classification can help organize visual evidence and present a preliminary indication, but predictions must be interpreted within the limits of training data, image quality, crop coverage, and field conditions. Prior work has demonstrated the technical potential of plant-leaf image learning [1], [2].')
para('AgriPredict combines crop-specific image classification with explicit image-quality handling, uncertain outcomes, disease information, and weather context. Its purpose is to support a farmer’s next step: capture a clearer image, review a preliminary result, consult context, or seek local expert advice. It is a decision-support prototype, not a substitute for professional diagnosis.')
h('1.2 Project Context and Motivation',2); para('A classifier may produce a confident label from a blurred image, the wrong crop, or a visual domain unlike its training data. AgriPredict checks image quality before inference and supports an uncertain decision when model-specific reliability criteria fail. This reject option follows selective prediction, which trades prediction coverage against error among answered cases [3].')
h('1.3 Aim and Contributions',2); para('The aim is to build a crop advisory application linking leaf-image models with a reliability-aware user experience and supporting context. Contributions include reported training for ten crops, deployed inference for apple and maize, image quality checks, uncertain outcomes, authenticated prediction history, selected disease knowledge, and separate weather summaries. Fertilizer features are included in the team’s status inventory, subject to verification described in Chapter 4.')
h('1.4 Report Organization',2); para('Chapter 2 reviews research and defines scope. Chapter 3 describes methodology and crop status. Chapter 4 documents implementation. Chapter 5 reports available evidence and missing experiments. Chapter 6 concludes and proposes future work.'); br()
h('Chapter 2  Literature Review'); h('2.1 Literature Survey',2); para('Hughes and Salathé introduced PlantVillage as an open collection of curated crop-leaf images intended to support mobile diagnostics [1]. Mohanty, Hughes, and Salathé demonstrated deep-learning classification using this dataset [2]. These studies established feasibility, but performance on curated images is not evidence of farmer-field accuracy.')
para('Residual networks use identity shortcuts to train deep visual representations [4]. AgriPredict apple artifacts describe a ResNet50 model with self-attention; other crop models have their own notebook/package implementations and should not be assumed to share that architecture. Selective prediction formalizes abstention when a model should not answer [3]. Confidence alone may be unreliable under distribution shift, so coverage-risk and OOD tests are appropriate.')
para('Open-Meteo documents location-based forecast variables such as temperature, humidity, precipitation, and wind [5]. Weather can contextualize an advisory, but crop-specific risk and treatment recommendations require agronomic validation. The current service presents general screening, not prescriptions.')
table(['Study','Contribution','Relevance / limit'],[['Hughes and Salathé [1]','Open crop image repository','Curated images may differ from field capture.'],['Mohanty et al. [2]','Deep learning on PlantVillage','Demonstrates feasibility, not field validity.'],['Geifman and El-Yaniv [3]','Selective classifier with reject option','Motivates abstention and coverage-risk reporting.'],['He et al. [4]','Residual learning','Background for ResNet feature extractors.'],['Open-Meteo [5]','Forecast API documentation','Weather context, not agronomic diagnosis.']])
h('2.2 Problem Statement',2); para('How can a farmer-facing application use crop-specific leaf-image models while reducing the risk of presenting unreliable predictions as certain, and provide disease and weather context without implying that weather is part of the image classifier?')
h('2.3 Objectives',2)
for x in ['Develop and document classifiers for ten reported crops.','Provide crop selection, image submission, results, and history in a web application.','Reject unsuitable images and communicate retake or uncertain outcomes.','Integrate model packages through a backend registry and adapters.','Present disease and weather context only where supported.','Document fertilizer modules with implementation evidence.','Evaluate classification, abstention, latency, and application behavior reproducibly.']: para('• '+x)
h('2.4 Scope of Project',2); para('The checked repository implements React/Vite and FastAPI, authentication, image checks, enabled apple and maize adapters, apple/maize disease knowledge, prediction persistence, and weather endpoints. The team reports ten trained crops and seven hosted models; those project-reported states are not equivalent to live application support. The system does not claim universal recognition, laboratory confirmation, field-validated treatment advice, or a multimodal disease model. Crop identity validation is marked not run. Fertilizer modules are team-reported but not identifiable in this source snapshot.'); br()
h('Chapter 3  Methodology'); h('3.1 System Requirements and Design',2); para('Functional requirements are authentication, supported-crop selection, image checks, inference, recorded outcomes, and contextual results. Non-functional requirements include model-version traceability, clear uncertain behavior, and separating service errors from model predictions. A registry resolves crop identifiers to enabled metadata and adapters; a predictor manager loads the model package; repositories store prediction and user data; weather and knowledge services remain separate.')
h('3.2 Model Development and Crop Coverage',2); para('Per project status supplied for this report, models are trained for ten crops. Available notebooks include apple, maize, grapes, tea, potato, cotton, and wheat; some of the reported training artifacts are outside the current checkout. The first seven listed project crops are reported hosted, while groundnut, okra, and pumpkin remain notebook-stage. Only apple and maize have enabled metadata and adapters in the checked application.')
table(['Crop','Training status','Hosting / artifact reported','Live inference'],[[c,'Trained',('Hugging Face' if c in ['Apple','Maize','Grapes','Tea','Potato','Cotton','Sugarcane'] else 'Notebook'),'Enabled' if c in ['Apple','Maize'] else 'Not registered'] for c in ['Apple','Maize','Grapes','Tea','Potato','Cotton','Sugarcane','Groundnut','Okra','Pumpkin']])
para('Apple and maize metadata identify model ID, version, framework, adapter, and package location. Hosted files are fetched at runtime. Hosting does not itself establish app support: metadata, an adapter, class alignment, validation, and user-interface integration are also needed.')
h('3.3 Reliability-Aware Prediction Workflow',2); table(['Stage','Operation','Outcome'],[['Select','Choose crop and image','Check enabled registry.'],['Validate','Type, size, decode, dimensions, brightness, blur','Retake outcome if checks fail.'],['Infer','Run crop-specific adapter','Return class and reliability signals.'],['Decide','Apply adapter criteria','ACCEPT or UNCERTAIN.'],['Contextualize','Knowledge lookup and separate weather request','Show context after inference.'],['Persist','Save record when requested','History for authenticated user.']])
para('The validator checks content type, size, decoding, minimum dimensions, brightness/overexposure, and blur. A failure returns a retake message before inference. The prediction route records plant and crop validation as not run. The apple adapter normalizes remote output and decision state; the user-facing response omits internal scores. Thresholds and per-class results should be reported from model-specific evaluation artifacts.')
h('3.4 Advisory and Data Handling',2); para('Disease knowledge is schema-validated JSON for apple and maize. A separate language-model layer can explain structured outputs. Weather is fetched by location through Open-Meteo; generic spraying and disease-weather summaries are not crop-specific diagnoses or chemical instructions. Weather follows inference and is not fused with image features.')
para('The team reports fertilizer recommendation and calculator modules. The current checkout has recommendation-record database models but no identifiable fertilizer calculation service, dataset, frontend screen, or endpoint. This report therefore records the modules as reported, with implementation/integration unverified. A final submission should include inputs, units, formulas or sources, crop/soil assumptions, safeguards, and screenshots/source paths. Prediction records include user, crop, model/version, decision, quality status, validation and prediction data, processing time, and storage metadata; the shown persistence call sets training consent false.'); br()
h('Chapter 4  Implementation'); h('4.1 Software Architecture',2); para('The React client sends authenticated requests to FastAPI. The backend validates and routes requests, invokes model adapters, and coordinates persistence and advisory services. Model packages are fetched from the configured Hugging Face repository. Weather is retrieved independently.')
table(['Layer','Technology','Responsibility'],[['Presentation','React, Vite','Scan, dashboard, result, history, weather, account views.'],['API','FastAPI','Protected prediction, history, weather, database routes.'],['Inference','PyTorch, registry, adapters','Resolve metadata, load package, normalize output.'],['Validation','Image-quality validator','Check image format and visual suitability.'],['Advisory','JSON knowledge, LLM, weather service','Explanation and separate weather context.'],['Persistence','SQLAlchemy repositories, configured services','Store users, predictions, and related records.']])
h('4.2 Backend Services',2); para('The prediction endpoint accepts crop, language, history preference, and image. It checks that the crop is enabled, validates type and size, performs quality checks, and invokes inference. Quality failures return before the model is called. Adapter and package-manager code isolate model loading from API handling. Lazy loading reduces idle resource use.')
para('The disease-knowledge service validates JSON and indexes by crop/class. The weather decision service presents generic screening with disclaimers. Prediction history links records to authenticated users. Database and external storage settings depend on deployment configuration and should be documented for the final demonstration.')
h('4.3 Web Application',2); para('The React/Vite client includes dashboard, scan, result, history, weather, login, registration, profile, and settings pages. API helpers submit images with authentication. Weather functions request browser location and display weather and spraying-condition summaries. Reliability status and messages explain uncertain results without exposing every internal score.')
h('4.4 Model Packaging and Deployment',2); para('Apple and maize metadata point to version 1.0.0 packages in Hugging Face repository viraj1920/plant-disease-models and mark both enabled with lazy loading. The project reports five additional hosted crops, but they are not enabled in this application snapshot. Hosting, integration, and testing are separate statuses.')
h('4.5 Feature Status',2); table(['Feature','Status','Evidence basis'],[['Ten crop training','Reported complete','Project status supplied; artifacts in checkout cover only some crops.'],['Seven hosted models','Reported hosted','Project status supplied; registry metadata only apple/maize.'],['Live disease inference','Integrated for apple/maize','Enabled metadata and adapters.'],['Image-quality checks','Integrated','Prediction route and validator.'],['Disease knowledge','Integrated for apple/maize','Validated JSON and service.'],['Weather context','Integrated separately','Weather client and decision service.'],['Fertilizer recommendation','Reported; integration unverified','No dedicated code identified in snapshot.'],['Fertilizer calculator','Reported; integration unverified','No dedicated code identified in snapshot.'],['Groundnut, okra, pumpkin','Notebook stage','Not registered for inference.']]); br()
h('Chapter 5  Experimental Results'); h('5.1 Evidence Boundaries',2); para('Training, hosting, application integration, functional testing, and field validation are distinct claims. Metrics are reported only where an evaluation artifact is available. The apple figures below are internal results reported in the repository README, not recomputed here. Test split size, class balance, image provenance, and confidence intervals should accompany a final results table if available.')
h('5.2 Apple Model Results',2); para('The README reports 96.22% internal test accuracy, macro-F1 0.9676, and OOD AUROC 0.9631 for an eight-class ResNet50 plus self-attention apple model. These results are not guarantees of field performance. OOD AUROC applies only to the evaluated in/out samples and does not establish reliability for every unseen crop, disease, camera, or environment.')
table(['Metric','Reported value','Interpretation / limit'],[['Internal test accuracy','96.22%','Repository-reported; test set detail absent here.'],['Macro-F1','0.9676','Per-class values needed to find weak classes.'],['OOD AUROC','0.9631','OOD definition and sample counts should be included.']])
h('5.3 Functional Behavior',2); para('Source inspection confirms code paths for unsupported-crop rejection, invalid type/oversize rejection, image-quality retake decisions, inference for enabled crops, uncertain outcomes from the adapter, and optional history persistence. This is an implementation review, not a claim that a complete acceptance-test suite or production deployment passed. Runtime crop coverage is two. Maize metrics and deployment class alignment need further documentation.')
h('5.4 Limitations and Evaluation Plan',2)
for x in ['Curated or internally split images may overstate performance on farmer field images.','Per-class metrics, confusion matrices, sample counts, confidence intervals, and independent field validation are missing for the broader model set.','Plant/crop identity checks are not run in the current prediction route.','Weather rules are general demonstration thresholds, not validated disease or pesticide advice.','Fertilizer implementation and integration cannot be verified from this checkout.','No measured latency, throughput, uptime, or mobile usability result is reported.']: para('• '+x)
table(['Dimension','Measure','Evidence needed'],[['Classification','Accuracy, macro-F1, per-class precision/recall','Frozen test split, counts, version, preprocessing.'],['Abstention','Coverage-risk, accepted error, rejection rates','OOD and in-domain set; fixed thresholds.'],['Image quality','Retake precision/recall by failure type','Labeled quality set and threshold protocol.'],['External validity','Performance by device, source, region, stage','Expert-labelled field images and governance.'],['Performance','Median/p95 model and end-to-end latency','Hardware, warm/cold start, concurrency.'],['Usability','Task completion and message comprehension','User study protocol and participant details.']]); br()
h('Chapter 6  Conclusion and Future Scope'); h('6.1 Conclusion',2); para('AgriPredict brings crop-specific image inference, quality checks, uncertain outcomes, disease knowledge, weather context, and user history into a web application. The checked repository integrates apple and maize; the team reports training ten crops and hosting seven, but those counts are not live support. Available apple metrics are internal results and do not establish field reliability. The design’s key feature is to withhold a diagnosis when reliability conditions fail and guide the user toward a next step.')
h('6.2 Future Scope',2)
for x in ['Integrate hosted models after validating preprocessing, class alignment, thresholds, package metadata, and UI behavior.','Package and independently evaluate groundnut, okra, and pumpkin models.','Publish complete per-crop reports with confusion matrices, class-wise metrics, OOD protocol, coverage-risk, and latency.','Collect consented field images across devices, locations, cultivars, lighting, and disease stages with expert labels.','Enable crop identity checks and improve capture guidance.','Review crop- and region-specific advice with authoritative agricultural sources and domain experts.','Verify fertilizer modules, documenting inputs, units, calculations, sources, assumptions, and safety checks.','Monitor model-package integrity, API errors, service availability, and privacy-aware feedback.']: para('• '+x)
h('6.3 Final Remarks',2); para('AgriPredict provides a foundation for accessible crop advisory. Its value depends on matching model claims to evidence, keeping uncertain outcomes clear, and validating the complete system against images and decisions encountered in practice.')
br(); h('REFERENCES')
for x in ['[1] D. P. Hughes and M. Salathé, “An open access repository of images on plant health to enable the development of mobile disease diagnostics,” arXiv:1511.08060, 2015. [Online]. Available: https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.08060','[2] S. P. Mohanty, D. P. Hughes, and M. Salathé, “Using deep learning for image-based plant disease detection,” Frontiers in Plant Science, vol. 7, Art. no. 1419, 2016, doi: 10.3389/fpls.2016.01419.','[3] Y. Geifman and R. El-Yaniv, “SelectiveNet: A deep neural network with an integrated reject option,” in Proc. 36th Int. Conf. Machine Learning, PMLR, vol. 97, 2019, pp. 2151–2159. [Online]. Available: https://proceedings.mlr.press/v97/geifman19a.html','[4] K. He, X. Zhang, S. Ren, and J. Sun, “Deep residual learning for image recognition,” in Proc. IEEE Conf. Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), 2016, pp. 770–778, doi: 10.1109/CVPR.2016.90.','[5] Open-Meteo, “Weather Forecast API documentation.” [Online]. Available: https://open-meteo.com/en/docs. Accessed: Oct. 18, 2026.','[6] AgriPredict project repository, README, backend and frontend source, model metadata, and notebooks, accessed Oct. 18, 2026. Internal project materials.']: para(x)
h('APPENDIX A  PROJECT ARTIFACTS REVIEWED'); para('Implementation status was checked against the repository README, model registry and adapters, prediction API, image validator, disease knowledge and weather services, frontend pages, apple/maize metadata, and notebooks in the checkout. Crop training/hosting inventory and fertilizer modules were supplied by the project team. Claims not corroborated in the source tree are labeled reported or unverified.')
